"Hedonism (Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy) (https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hedonism/)\nciteturn8view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn5view2\",\"lineno\":189}); Total lines: 309\nL185: Why would anyone think, even for a minute, that hedonism is a plausible theory of value? Even if we focus very narrowly, just on those mental states of ours that arguably are instances of pleasure or have pleasure as a higher-order property – contentment, delight, ecstasy, elation, enjoyment, euphoria, exhilaration, exultation, gladness, gratification, gratitude, joy, liking, love, relief, satisfaction, Schadenfreude, tranquility, and so on – each of these mental states or events or properties also has one or more non-hedonic properties that contribute to its importance.\nL186: Beyond pleasure, our mental lives are full of significant and diverse thoughts, perceptions, emotions, imaginings, wishes, and so on. These engage with massively plural and diverse items in ourselves, in others, in myriad aspects of the non-human world, and in the infinities of contingent future possibility. This is true also of our relationships with ourselves and with others, and with multiple aspects of the wider world.\nL187: It is true also of our agency – our deliberations, choices, plans, intentions, and so forth. In the light of such reflections, an incredulous stare might be thought an apt response to a profession of belief in ethical hedonism. This incredulous stare argument is far from decisive, but perhaps it should disrupt any complacent presumption in favour of hedonism.\nL188: Many well-known criticisms of hedonism can reasonably be interpreted as non-necessity objections. A short survey of some of the more significant of these follows.\nL189: Plato pointed out that if your life is just one of pleasure then it would not even include any recollection of pleasure; nor any distinct thought that you were pleased, even when you were pleased. His conclusion was that “your life would be the life, not of a man, but of an oyster” (Philebus 21a). Similarly, on J.S. Mill's account of him at least (Mill: ch. 2), Carlyle held that hedonism is a “doctrine worthy only of swine”.\nL190: Nozick (1971) and Nagel (1970) present schematic descriptions of lives that have all the appearance but none of the reality of self-understanding, achievement, loving relationships, self-directedness, and so on, alongside lives that have these appearances and also the corresponding realities. On the face of it, hedonism is committed to the hedonic equality and thus the equal value of these lives.\nL191: Commenting on his more fantastical and more famous ‘experience machine’ case, Nozick added further detail, claiming that it is also good in itself “to do certain things, and not just have the experience [as if] of doing them”, “to be a certain way, to be a certain sort of person” and not just to be an “indeterminate blob” floating in a tank, and “to make a difference in the world” rather than merely to appear to oneself to do so.\nL192: He concluded: “something matters to us in addition to experience” (Nozick 1974: 43–44).\nL193: Consider further the idea that actually having certain relationships with oneself (e.g., relations of self-understanding) and with others (e.g., mutual relations of interpersonal love) matters, in addition to the value of any experience one has that is just as if one has such relationships. The thought here is that the motto ‘also connect’ expresses something important, even if novelist E.M. Forster's more ambitious ‘only connect’ (Forster: ch. 33) was an exaggeration.\nL194: In a famous case description, Moore argued that a world with beauty but without its contemplation, and indeed without any mental states whatever, is better than a world that is “simply one heap of filth” (Moore: sec. 50; contrast Sidgwick: 114). If Moore is right about this ‘beauty and the filth’ case, then pleasure is not necessary for value.\nL195: W.D. Ross (138) considered two worlds that are equals both hedonically and in character terms. In one world, the virtuous have the pleasure and the vicious have the pain, while in the other the vicious have the pleasure and the virtuous have the pain. To help secure across all plausible accounts of the nature of pleasure the ‘equality of pleasure’ that is central to this case comparison, suppose that in each world the same pleasures are taken in the same objects.\nL196: Pleasure is equal across these two worlds, but Ross argues that the well-matched world is better than the mis-matched world. If he is right, then this is a case of ‘same pleasure, different value’, and thereby also a case in which difference of pleasure is not necessary for difference of value.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFailure to detect changes to people during a real-world interaction (https://gwern.net/doc/psychology/cognitive-bias/illusion-of-depth/1998-simons.pdf)\nciteturn8view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 6; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn7view4\",\"lineno\":20}); Total lines: 382\nL0@P0: Copyright 1998 Psychonomic Society, Inc. 644\nL1@P0: Psychonomic Bulletin & Review\nL2@P0: 1998, 5 (4), 644-649\nL3@P0: Despite our impression that we retain the visual details\nL4@P0: of our surroundings from one view to the next, we are\nL5@P0: surprisingly unable to detect changes to such details. Re\u0002cently, experiments from a number of laboratories have\nL6@P0: shown that people fail to detect substantial changes to pho\u0002tographs of objects and real-world scenes when the abil\u0002ity to detect retinal differences is eliminated (Blackmore,\nL7@P0: Brelstaff, Nelson, & Troscianko, 1995; Grimes, 1996;\nL8@P0: Henderson, 1997; McConkie & Currie, 1996; O’Regan,\nL9@P0: Deubel, Clark, & Rensink, 1997; Pashler, 1988; Phillips,\nL10@P0: 1974; Rensink, O’Regan, & Clark, 1997; Simons, 1996;\nL11@P0: for a review see Simons & Levin, 1997). That is, when\nL12@P0: retinally localizable information signaling a change is\nL13@P0: masked by an eye movement or a flashed blank screen,\nL14@P0: observers have difficulty detecting changes to the visual\nL15@P0: details of a scene. These findings of “change blindness”\nL16@P0: suggest that observers lack a precise visual representa\u0002tion of their world from one view to the next. Although\nL17@P0: we have known for some time that memory for scenes is\nL18@P0: often distorted, sometimes quite sparse, subject to sugges\u0002tions, and influenced by expectations and goals (Bartlett,\nL19@P0: 1932/1977; Brewer & Treyens, 1981; Loftus, 1979; Nick\u0002erson & Adams, 1979), studies of change blindness sug\u0002gest that such details may not be retained even from one\nL20@P0: instant to the next, a claim that is consistent with earlier\nL21@P0: studies of the integration of information from successive\nL22@P0: fixations (Bridgeman & Mayer, 1983; Dennett, 1991;\nL23@P0: Hochberg, 1986; Irwin, 1991; McConkie & Currie, 1996;\nL24@P0: Pashler, 1988; Rayner & Pollatsek, 1992).\nL25@P0: Given the richness of our visual world, it is perhaps\nL26@P0: unsurprising that we cannot represent all the visual de\u0002tails of every object and instead must focus on a few im\u0002portant objects. Recent models of attention have argued\nL27@P0: that observers can fully represent the details of only a few\nL28@P0: centrally attended objects in a scene. For example, models\nL29@P0: based on object files (e.g., Treisman, 1993) suggest that\nL30@P0: we can simultaneously represent several distinct objects\nL31@P0: in our environment, updating our representations for\nL32@P0: changes in their properties and features. Such models sug\u0002gest the possibility that representations of centrally at\u0002tended objects are relatively detailed even if those for pe\u0002ripheral objects are not.\nL33@P0: The authors contributed equally to this report, and authorship order\nL34@P0: was determined arbitrarily. Thanks to Leon Rozenblit, Carter Smith,\nL35@P0: Julia Noland, and Joy Beck for helping to carry out the experiments and\nL36@P0: to Linda Hermer for reading an earlier draft of the manuscript. D.J.S.\nL37@P0: was supported by NSF and Jacob K. Javits fellowships, and parts of this\nL38@P0: research appeared in his doctoral thesis. Correspondence should be ad\u0002dressed to D. J. Simons, Department of Psychology, Harvard University,\nL39@P0: 820 William James Hall, 33 Kirkland St., Cambridge, MA 02138 (e\u0002mail: dsimons@wjh.harvard.edu) or D. T. Levin, Department of Psy\u0002chology, Kent State University, P.O. Box 5190, Kent, OH 44242-0001\nL40@P0: (e-mail: dlevin@kent.edu).\nL41@P0: BRIEF REPORTS\nL42@P0: Failure to detect changes to people\nL43@P0: during a real-world interaction\nL44@P0: DANIEL J. SIMONS\nL45@P0: Harvard University, Cambridge, Massachusetts\nL46@P0: and\nL47@P0: DANIEL T. LEVIN\nL48@P0: Kent State University, Kent, Ohio\nL49@P0: Recent research on change detection has documented surprising failures to detect visual changes oc\u0002curring between views of a scene, suggesting the possibility that visual representations contain few de\u0002tails. Although these studies convincingly demonstrate change blindness for objects in still images and\nL50@P0: motion pictures, they may not adequately assess the capacity to represent objects in the real world.\nL51@P0: Here we examine and reject the possibility that change blindness in previous studies resulted from pas\u0002sive viewing of 2-D displays. In one experiment, an experimenter initiated a conversation with a pedes\u0002trian, and during the interaction, he was surreptitiously replaced by a different experimenter. Only half\nL52@P0: of the pedestrians detected the change. Furthermore, successful detection depended on social group\nL53@P0: membership; pedestrians from the same social group as the experimenters detected the change but\nL54@P0: those from a different social group did not. A second experiment further examined the importance of\nL55@P0: this effect of social group. Provided that the meaning of the scene is unchanged, changes to attended\nL56@P0-1: objects can escape detection even when they occur during a natural, real-world interaction. The dis\u0002cussion provides a set of guidelines and suggestions for future research on change blindness.CHANGE DETECTION 645\nL57@P1: A recent series of studies directly examined the role of\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn8view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://pubs.usgs.gov/publication/ofr8797\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNational Institute for Basic Biology / News (https://www.nibb.ac.jp/en/pressroom/news/2020/10/06.html)\nciteturn8view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.nibb.ac.jp/en/pressroom/news/2020/10/06.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 274\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nL.A. Math: Romance, Crime, and Mathematics in the City of Angels - Supplementary Materials (https://assets.press.princeton.edu/releases/m2-10559.pdf)\nciteturn8view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 242; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn7view5\",\"pattern\":\"Hilbert's Hotel\"}); Total lines: 4948\nL2187@P104: A but not in B. That is, A \\ B = A ∩ B', or, using set-builder notation, A \\ B =\nL2188@P104: { x : x ∈ A and x ∉ B }. In Example 5 and 6 (below), we have used the idea of A \\ B before we had\nL2189@P104: specifically defined it.\nL2190@P104: Example 6 - If A = { dog, pig, rat, cat, yak } and B = { rat, cat }, what is A \\ B ? What is B \\ A ?\nL2191@P104: Solution: A \\ B is the set of all things belonging to A but not to B, which is { dog, pig, yak }.\nL2192@P104: B \\ A is the set of all things belonging to B but not to A, which is ∅ . █\nL2193@P104:  We have mentioned the analogy between arithmetic and set theory. There is a temptation to\nL2194@P104: regard the set-theoretic difference as being analogous to subtraction, because when we construct the\nL2195@P104: set A \\ B we are 'taking away' the things in B from A. However, this analogy should not be taken\nL2196@P104: too far. For example, if the number b is larger than the number a, the difference a - b is negative. If\nL2197@P104: the set B is 'larger' than the set A (i.e. B ⊇ A), the set-theoretic difference A \\ B = ∅, the empty set.\nL2198@P104: This situation occurs in the second part of Example 7. There is no concept in set theory analogous\nL2199@P104: to negative numbers.\nL2200@P104-105: Section 3 - The Nature of Infinity106\nL2201@P105:  We start this section by discussing a couple of examples which indicate some of the\nL2202@P105: counterintuitive properties of infinite sets.\nL2203@P105: Hilbert's Hotel: Where They Never Say, \"Sorry, We're Full Up\"\nL2204@P105:  In an ordinary hotel (one with 20, 200, or 2000 rooms), once every room is full, a late-arriving\nL2205@P105: guest cannot obtain an unoccupied room. David Hilbert, one of the greatest mathematicians of the\nL2206@P105: early twentieth century, concocted the following example, which is called Hilbert's Hotel in his\nL2207@P105: honor.\nL2208@P105:  Imagine a hotel with an infinite number of rooms, one room for every positive whole number.\nL2209@P105: Suppose that every room is occupied, and a guest arrives looking for an empty room. Rather than\nL2210@P105: turn the guest (and the accompanying cash) out in the cold, the hotel manager simply moves the\nL2211@P105: occupant of Room 1 to Room 2, the occupant of Room 2 to Room 3, the occupant of Room 3 to\nL2212@P105: Room 4, etc. After all this moving is done, every former occupant still has a room, but Room 1 is\nL2213@P105: now free, and the new guest moves in!\nL2214@P105:  Later in the evening, the hotel is still full, and an infinite number of new guests arrive. Not to\nL2215@P105: worry. The manager gives each new guest a number, just as one would at a store where you have to\nL2216@P105: wait in line. While the guests are in the bar, the manager moves the occupant of Room 1 to Room\nL2217@P105: 2, the occupant of Room 2 to Room 4, the occupant of Room 3 to Room 6, etc. All the previous\nL2218@P105: occupants are now in even-numbered rooms. The odd-numbered rooms are now vacant, so new\nL2219@P105: Guest 1 moves into Room 1, new Guest 2 moves into Room 3, new Guest 3 moves into Room 5,\nL2220@P105: and so on. Everyone is happy (especially the stockholders)!\nL2221@P105: Money for Nothing\nL2222@P105:  Suppose you have ten friends and no money. You talk five of them into loaning you $1 each, and\nL2223@P105: you loan $1 each to the other five. You have created five debtors and five creditors, but your net\nL2224@P105-106: worth hasn't changed -- you still have no money. 107\nL2225@P106:  Now let's suppose you still have no money, but you now have infinitely many friends, whom we\nL2226@P106: will number Friend 1, Friend 2, etc. You talk each of your odd-numbered friends into loaning you\nL2227@P106: $1, and you loan $1 to each of your even-numbered friends. You now decide to square accounts\nL2228@P106: with everyone, but you do it in an unusual way.\nL2229@P106:  Step 1 - Collect the $1 each owed to you by Friends 2 and 4, and pay $1 back to Friend 1. You\nL2230@P106: gain $1, which you deposit in the bank.\nL2231@P106:  Step 2 - Collect the $1 each owed to you by Friends 6 and 8, and pay $1 back to Friend 3. You\nL2232@P106: gain $1, which you deposit in the bank.\nL2233@P106:  Step 3 - Collect the $1 each owed to you by Friends 10 and 12, and pay $1 back to Friend 5. You\nL2234@P106: gain $1, which you deposit in the bank.\nL2235@P106:  You continue this process. After you have finished, you are all square with everyone -- you have\nL2236@P106: paid back every dollar you owe, and no one owes you any money. You also have infinitely many\nL2237@P106: dollars in the bank! Money for nothing – except a lot of work!\nL2238@P106: Countable Sets\nL2239@P106:  The above two examples illustrate that the rules which govern manipulations with infinite sets\nL2240@P106: must be very different from the ones with which we are familiar. When we first learn the properties\nL2241@P106: of numbers, we learn about \"three-ness\" by discovering that all sets with three elements in them can\nL2242@P106: be placed in one-to-one correspondence with each other. Moreover, this idea of one-to-one\nL2243@P106: correspondence can be used to define the ideas of 'greater than' or 'less than'. Suppose we have two\nL2244@P106: sets, one consisting of children, and one consisting of cookies. If we can give one cookie to each\nL2245@P106: child and at the end of doing so each child gets a cookie and no cookies are left over, the set of\nL2246@P106: children and the set of cookies have exactly the same number of items in them. Mathematicians say\nL2247@P106-107: that these two sets have the same cardinality. If we have cookies left over, the set of cookies has 108\nL2248@P107: greater cardinality than the set of children. Similarly, if some children don't get cookies, then the set\nL2249@P107: of children has greater cardinality than the set of cookies.\nL2250@P107:  The example of Hilbert's Hotel illustrates that, for infinite sets, one-to-one correspondence will\nL2251@P107: not enable us to distinguish 'less than' from 'precisely the same number as.' Indeed, the concepts of\nL2252@P107: 'less than' and 'precisely the same number as' require careful definition in the context of infinite sets.\nL2253@P107: For instance, the set { 2, 3, 4, ... } appears to have 'fewer' elements than the set { 1, 2, 3, ... }.\nL2254@P107: However, they can be placed in one-to-one correspondence by means of the following arrangement.\nL2255@P107:  2 3 4 ....\nL2256@P107:    \nL2257@P107:  1 2 3 ....\nL2258@P107:  Therefore, the sets { 2, 3, 4, ... } and { 1, 2, 3, ... } have the same cardinality.\nL2259@P107:  All of the infinite sets we have examined are subsets of the positive integers, and they can all be\nL2260@P107: put in one-to-one correspondence with the integers (and also with each other). Such sets are called\nL2261@P107: countable. This raises a question: is it possible to describe an infinite set S of people who cannot be\nL2262@P107: housed in Hilbert's Hotel? This question was originally answered by Georg Cantor (who suffered a\nL2263@P107: mental breakdown in the process – you hear about situations such as this with artists but rarely with\nL2264@P107: scientists).\nL2265@P107: An Uncountable Set\nL2266@P107:  To describe a set S of people who cannot be housed in Hilbert's Hotel, imagine that each\nL2267@P107: individual in the set S has a name which can be written out using an infinite sequence of letters and\nL2268@P107: blanks. The first letter of Elvis Presley's name is E, the second is L, ... , the twelfth is E, the\nL2269@P107-108: thirteenth is Y, and all the other letters, such as the eighty-first or three-millionth or 109\nL2270@P108: nine-quadrillionth, is blank. We will show that Hilbert's Hotel is not large enough to contain the set\nL2271@P108: of people with all possible infinitely-long names.\nL2272@P108:  To see this, imagine that Elvis Presley is in Room 1, Jimmy Hoffa is in Room 2, Amelia Earhart\nL2273@P108: is in Room 3, Judge Crater is in Room 4, and every person in the set has been placed in a room. To\nL2274@P108: show that this cannot have been done, we shall exhibit the name of a person who cannot have been\nL2275@P108: given a room in the hotel. We shall construct that person's name using the initials of the King, the\nL2276@P108: letters E and P.\nL2277@P108:  Let's look at the guest register.\nL2278@P108:  Room 1 - ELVIS PRESLEY (the first letter is underlined)\nL2279@P108:  Room 2 - JIMMY HOFFA (the second letter is underlined)\nL2280@P108:  Room 3 - AMELIA EARHART (the third letter is underlined)\nL2281@P108:  Room 4 - JUDGE CRATER (the fourth letter is underlined)\nL2282@P108:  ... (and so on)\nL2283@P108:  We construct our mystery guest's name one letter at a time, according to the following rule: if the\nL2284@P108: Nth letter of the inhabitant of Room N is an E, then the Nth letter of our mystery guest's name will\nL2285@P108: be a P. If the Nth letter of the inhabitant of Room N is not an E, then the Nth letter of our mystery\nL2286@P108: guest's name will be an E. We have underlined the first letter in the first name in the diagram above,\nL2287@P108: the second letter in the second name, etc.\nL2288@P108:  Since Elvis Presley is in Room 1, and the first letter of his name is E, the first letter of our\nL2289@P108: mystery guest's name is P. Since Jimmy Hoffa is in Room 2, and the second letter of his name is I\nL2290@P108: (not E), the second letter of our mystery guest's name is E. Since Amelia Earhart is in Room 3, and\nL2291@P108-109: the third letter of her name is E, the third letter of our mystery guest's name is P. Since Judge Crater 110\nL2292@P109: is in Room 4, and the fourth letter of his name is G (not E), the fourth letter of our mystery guest's\nL2293@P109: name is E. So the mystery guest's name is PEPE ... .\nL2294@P109:  In what room is our mystery guest? Not in Room 1, since the first letter of his name (P) is\nL2295@P109: different from the first letter (E) of Elvis Presley, the occupant of Room 1. Not in Room 2, either,\nL2296@P109: since the second letter of his name (E) is different from the second letter (I) of Jimmy Hoffa, the\nL2297@P109: occupant of Room 2. Our mystery guest cannot be found in Room 3, since the third letter of his\nL2298@P109: name (P) is different from the third letter (E) of Amelia Earhart, the occupant of Room 3. Nor is he,\nL2299@P109: or she, or it, in Room 4, since the fourth letter of his, or her, or its name (E) is different from the\nL2300@P109: fourth letter (G) of Judge Crater, the occupant of Room 4. In fact, our mystery guest is nowhere to\nL2301@P109: be found in Hilbert's Hotel, since the Nth letter of his name differs from the Nth letter of the name of\nL2302@P109: the guest in Room N!\nL2303@P109:  This extremely ingenious proof is known as Cantor's Diagonal Argument, and is one of the\nL2304@P109: standard techniques used in proving results about infinite sets.\nL2305@P109:  Any infinite set which cannot be put in one-to-one correspondence with the integers, such as the\nL2306@P109: set of all infinitely-long names, is called uncountable. The set of real numbers, which is essentially\nL2307@P109: the set of all infinitely-long names using the alphabet of digits 0,1,2,…,9 rather than the alphabet of\nL2308@P109-110: Roman letters A,B,…,Z, is uncountable.111\nL2309@P110: Chapter 8 – The Chinese Restaurant Principle; Combinatorics\nL2310@P110: Introduction\nL2311@P110:  The need for counting techniques arises because it is often necessary to count very large numbers\nL2312@P110: of items. After all, if there are only five or so items in a set, one just counts them. On the other\nL2313@P110: hand, if there are several hundred items in a set, counting them is likely to take some time, and there\nL2314@P110: is the possibility of a mistake. It may even happen that the number of items we wish to count is so\nL2315@P110: large that we would never be able to do it directly.\nL2316@P110:  We have already discussed one of the most important counting procedures, the Fundamental\nL2317@P110: Counting Principle, which is summarized in the formula\nL2318@P110: N( A ∪ B ) = N(A) + N(B) - N( A ∩ B )\nL2319@P110:  In this chapter, we shall investigate counting techniques based on successive choices. Many\nL2320@P110: decisions can be viewed as a procedure based on successive choices. When we order dinner in a\nL2321@P110: restaurant, we successively choose an appetizer, an entree, and a dessert. When a basketball coach\nL2322@P110: selects a starting line-up, he chooses the center, the two forwards, and the two guards.\nL2323@P110:  Mathematics started with the problem of counting, and counting problems still underlie many of\nL2324@P110: the most important areas of mathematics, such as probability, statistics, and decision theory. The\nL2325@P110: techniques we establish in this section will reappear frequently throughout the remainder of this\nL2326@P110-111: book.112\nL2327@P111: The Chinese Restaurant Principle\nL2328@P111:  The Chinese Restaurant Principle Pete referred to in the preceding story derives its name from the\nL2329@P111: following situation. A staple of Chinese restaurants has been the fixed-price meal, consisting of one\nL2330@P111: appetizer chosen from a list of many different appetizers, and one main course from a similar list. It\nL2331@P111: is traditional for the menu to present these choices in the form of two columns.\nL2332@P111:  Menu\nL2333@P111: Column A Column B\nL2334@P111: Won Ton Soup Sweet and Sour Pork\nL2335@P111: Spareribs Lemon Chicken\nL2336@P111: Rumaki Oyster Beef\nL2337@P111: Egg Roll Moo Goo Gai Pan\nL2338@P111: Dumplings Shrimp with Lobster Sauce\nL2339@P111:  Mixed Fried Noodles\nL2340@P111:  Pressed Duck\nL2341@P111:  For a fixed price, the diner gets to choose one dish from Column A, and one from Column B. An\nL2342@P111: obvious question now arises: how many different possible fixed-price meals can one have?\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n“Houston, We’ve Had a Problem” - NASA (https://www.nasa.gov/history/houston-weve-had-a-problem/)\nciteturn8view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn5view1\",\"pattern\":\"square\"}); Total lines: 430\nL325: An hour and a half later, with the Moon now more than 6,200 miles behind them, the astronauts fired the LM’s DPS engine for 4 minutes and 24 seconds to accelerate the spacecraft and return them to Earth 10 hours earlier and in the Pacific Ocean. Mission Control filled with many astronauts and managers, including NASA Administrator Thomas O. Paine, to observe the PC+2 maneuver. After completing the burn, the astronauts powered down Aquarius to conserve the spacecraft’s batteries.\nL326: One unpleasant side effect of the LM power down along with the total shutdown on the CM was cabin temperatures dropping to uncomfortable levels without the electronic equipment generating heat, making sleep more difficult.\nL327: cite208†Image: apollo_13_co2_system_test_in_vacuum_chamber cite209†Image: apollo_13_mcc_slayton_showing_lioh_adaptor cite210†Image: apollo_13_fd4_lioh_setup_swigert_and_lovell_hands L328: Left: Setup to use CM LiOH canisters in the LM during an altitude chamber test.\nL329: Middle: Slayton demonstrating CM LiOH canister setup to managers in Mission Control.\nL330: Right: Swigert (right) and Lovell assembling the CM LiOH setup in the LM Aquarius.\nL331: The next order of business involved rigging up a system to use the LiOH canisters from Odyssey that are square in shape with the system in Aquarius that uses round canisters. This provided the crew with adequate CO2 removal for the remainder of the flight. Brand informed the astronauts that a team was working on a procedure to solve this “square peg in a round hole” problem. Indeed, a team led by cite211†Robert E. “Ed” Smylie , Chief of the Crew Systems Division, his Deputy James V.\nL332: Correale and Test Director James C. “Jim” LeBlanc did exactly that, using only replicas of items the crew had onboard. The setup involved using a spacesuit’s liquid cooled garment and inlet hose, plastic bags, a cue card from the flight plan and of course duct tape to attach the CM canister to the LM’s outlet hose to pass air through to scrub the CO2. The engineers tested the setup in an altitude chamber and Capcom Kerwin called the procedure up to the crew.\nL333: Swigert and Lovell built two of the makeshift contraptions and within minutes after the astronauts installed the first one in Aquarius, CO_{2} levels dropped significantly.\nL334: cite212†Image: apollo_13_lovell_swigert_eating_in_lm cite213†Image: apollo_13_fd5_haise_in_lm L335: Left: Lovell (left) and Swigert eating in the LM during the Earthward coast.\nL336: Right: Haise in the LM during the Earthward coast.\nL337: Brand called up to the astronauts about the status of their consumables and all looked adequate to get them home safely. To reassure them further, Chief of Flight Crew Operations Donald K. “Deke” Slayton got on the Capcom loop, and knowing they hadn’t had much sleep since well before the accident, advised them, “We think you guys are in great shape, all the way around.\nL338: Why don’t you quit worrying and go to sleep.” Lovell responded, “Well, I think we just might do that – or part of us will,” a reference to the fact that one astronaut stayed awake on watch duty at all times since the accident. He and Swigert slept for about five hours in Odyssey with Haise keeping watch in Aquarius. After that exchange, Lousma relieved Brand on the Capcom console while Flight Director Windler’s Maroon Team of controllers took their respective consoles.\nL339: cite214†Image: apollo_13_fd5_lunney_99_hour_presser cite215†Image: apollo_13_fd5_mcc_griffin_windler L340: Left: Flight Director Lunney at a press conference during the Earthward coast.\nL341: Right: Flight Directors Griffin (left) and Windler during the Earthward coast.\nL342: The astronauts put the spacecraft in a Passive Thermal Control (PTC) or barbecue mode, rotating about once every 11 minutes along the longitudinal axis to evenly distribute temperatures. The spacecraft experienced a slight wobble, likely due to venting from one of the SM’s hydrogen tanks as they heated up during the sunlit part of the PTC rotation. Lunney’s Black Team of controllers came on to relieve Windler’s engineers and Kerwin came back in to replace Lousma at the Capcom console.\n"
